1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:03,460
Albert Einstein is the most
famous scientist who ever

2
00:00:03,470 --> 00:00:04,270
lived.

3
00:00:04,290 --> 00:00:07,560
But for thirty years, one man
kept telling him he was

4
00:00:07,570 --> 00:00:08,370
wrong.

5
00:00:08,667 --> 00:00:11,207
Their argument was about the
deepest question in physics:

6
00:00:11,827 --> 00:00:14,217
is the universe, at its smallest
scale,

7
00:00:14,227 --> 00:00:15,027
certain?

8
00:00:15,667 --> 00:00:16,867
Or is it ruled by chance?

9
00:00:17,833 --> 00:00:21,233
Einstein famously said: God does
not play dice.

10
00:00:22,033 --> 00:00:23,053
His rival's reply?

11
00:00:23,593 --> 00:00:25,273
Stop telling God what to do.

12
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:29,370
The man who challenged Einstein
was the Danish physicist

13
00:00:29,380 --> 00:00:30,600
Niels Bohr.

14
00:00:30,860 --> 00:00:33,660
And in the end, experiments
proved Bohr right.

15
00:00:35,317 --> 00:00:38,507
Bohr was born in Copenhagen in
eighteen eighty five,

16
00:00:38,517 --> 00:00:40,357
into a family of scholars.

17
00:00:41,117 --> 00:00:43,777
He was also a talented football
goalkeeper.

18
00:00:44,437 --> 00:00:47,197
His brother Harald played for
Denmark at the Olympics.

19
00:00:48,250 --> 00:00:50,750
In the early nineteen hundreds,
nobody understood atoms.

20
00:00:51,430 --> 00:00:54,620
Experiments showed a tiny, heavy
nucleus with

21
00:00:54,630 --> 00:00:57,780
electrons around it, but by the
known laws of

22
00:00:57,790 --> 00:00:58,590
physics,

23
00:00:58,810 --> 00:01:02,200
the electrons should spiral
inwards and crash in a

24
00:01:02,210 --> 00:01:03,450
fraction of a second.

25
00:01:04,250 --> 00:01:07,430
In nineteen thirteen, Bohr
proposed a bold fix.

26
00:01:08,130 --> 00:01:11,250
Electrons could only travel in
certain fixed orbits,

27
00:01:11,810 --> 00:01:12,890
like rungs on a ladder.

28
00:01:13,667 --> 00:01:16,917
When an electron jumped down
from a higher orbit to a lower

29
00:01:16,927 --> 00:01:17,727
one,

30
00:01:17,747 --> 00:01:20,547
it gave out a flash of light of
one exact colour.

31
00:01:21,333 --> 00:01:24,463
It explained the strange
bar-code of colours that
hydrogen

32
00:01:24,473 --> 00:01:26,213
gives off when it glows.

33
00:01:26,593 --> 00:01:29,671
Bohr won the Nobel Prize in
nineteen twenty two.

34
00:01:31,067 --> 00:01:33,147
But Bohr's model was only a
start.

35
00:01:33,807 --> 00:01:36,447
In the nineteen twenties, a
group of young physicists,

36
00:01:37,367 --> 00:01:40,567
many of them working at Bohr's
institute in Copenhagen,

37
00:01:41,347 --> 00:01:42,627
built a whole new theory:

38
00:01:43,307 --> 00:01:44,107
quantum mechanics.

39
00:01:44,750 --> 00:01:48,110
Bohr and Einstein first met in
Berlin in nineteen

40
00:01:48,120 --> 00:01:48,500
twenty,

41
00:01:48,510 --> 00:01:50,270
and liked each other at once.

42
00:01:51,130 --> 00:01:54,300
Einstein wrote that not often in
life had a person given him

43
00:01:54,310 --> 00:01:55,930
such joy just by being there.

44
00:01:56,583 --> 00:01:58,823
Years later, on a visit to
Copenhagen,

45
00:01:59,443 --> 00:02:02,833
they got so absorbed arguing on
a tram that they missed their

46
00:02:02,843 --> 00:02:03,613
stop,

47
00:02:03,623 --> 00:02:05,463
rode back, and missed it again.

48
00:02:06,250 --> 00:02:09,420
Quantum mechanics made
astonishingly accurate

49
00:02:09,430 --> 00:02:10,230
predictions.

50
00:02:10,790 --> 00:02:13,770
But it described the world in a
deeply strange way.

51
00:02:14,417 --> 00:02:17,807
Fire single particles, like
electrons, at a wall with

52
00:02:17,817 --> 00:02:20,907
two slits, and they land one by
one on the

53
00:02:20,917 --> 00:02:23,337
screen behind, like tiny
bullets.

54
00:02:24,250 --> 00:02:27,170
But as more arrive, they build
up a pattern of stripes,

55
00:02:27,750 --> 00:02:31,060
the kind of pattern only waves
make, as if each particle

56
00:02:31,070 --> 00:02:32,890
went through both slits at once.

57
00:02:33,750 --> 00:02:36,370
According to quantum mechanics,
before you look,

58
00:02:36,830 --> 00:02:39,310
a particle doesn't have one
definite position.

59
00:02:39,950 --> 00:02:41,150
It has only probabilities.

60
00:02:42,230 --> 00:02:45,540
Measurement forces a result, and
which result is

61
00:02:45,550 --> 00:02:46,850
down to pure chance.

62
00:02:47,750 --> 00:02:51,040
Werner Heisenberg showed that
you can't even know a

63
00:02:51,050 --> 00:02:54,340
particle's exact position and
its exact speed

64
00:02:54,350 --> 00:02:55,470
at the same time.

65
00:02:56,010 --> 00:02:57,750
This is the uncertainty
principle.

66
00:02:58,667 --> 00:03:00,167
Bohr embraced all of this.

67
00:03:00,887 --> 00:03:01,687
Einstein,

68
00:03:01,767 --> 00:03:03,767
who had helped to start quantum
physics,

69
00:03:04,287 --> 00:03:05,247
could not accept it.

70
00:03:05,833 --> 00:03:09,123
In nineteen oh five, Einstein
had shown that light

71
00:03:09,133 --> 00:03:12,363
comes in little packets of
energy, which we now call

72
00:03:12,373 --> 00:03:13,173
photons.

73
00:03:13,793 --> 00:03:15,853
That's what won him his own
Nobel Prize.

74
00:03:16,917 --> 00:03:18,367
But a universe run on chance?

75
00:03:18,377 --> 00:03:21,867
Einstein believed there must be
a deeper reality

76
00:03:21,877 --> 00:03:22,307
underneath,

77
00:03:22,317 --> 00:03:24,517
where everything had a definite
cause.

78
00:03:25,417 --> 00:03:28,867
In a letter in nineteen twenty
six, he wrote that he was

79
00:03:28,877 --> 00:03:30,837
convinced God does not throw
dice.

80
00:03:31,750 --> 00:03:32,550
He wasn't alone.

81
00:03:33,030 --> 00:03:33,830
Erwin Schrödinger,

82
00:03:34,350 --> 00:03:37,650
who had written quantum
mechanics' most famous equation,

83
00:03:38,030 --> 00:03:41,530
imagined a cat in a box that,
according to the theory,

84
00:03:41,850 --> 00:03:45,030
would be both alive and dead
until someone looked.

85
00:03:46,483 --> 00:03:49,513
The showdown came in October
nineteen twenty seven,

86
00:03:49,523 --> 00:03:52,043
at the Solvay Conference in
Brussels.

87
00:03:53,083 --> 00:03:56,273
It was perhaps the most famous
gathering in the history of

88
00:03:56,283 --> 00:03:57,083
science.

89
00:03:57,443 --> 00:04:00,613
Seventeen of the twenty nine
people in the official
photograph

90
00:04:00,623 --> 00:04:03,883
won a Nobel Prize, including
Marie Curie,

91
00:04:04,103 --> 00:04:04,903
who won two.

92
00:04:05,583 --> 00:04:08,063
The official topic was electrons
and photons.

93
00:04:08,983 --> 00:04:12,373
The real topic, everyone knew,
was whether quantum mechanics

94
00:04:12,383 --> 00:04:14,423
told the whole truth about
nature.

95
00:04:15,167 --> 00:04:16,327
Each morning at breakfast,

96
00:04:16,987 --> 00:04:20,157
Einstein would present a clever
thought experiment designed

97
00:04:20,167 --> 00:04:22,047
to break quantum mechanics.

98
00:04:23,083 --> 00:04:24,763
Bohr would worry about it all
day.

99
00:04:25,343 --> 00:04:27,583
And by dinner, he usually had an
answer.

100
00:04:29,233 --> 00:04:32,143
Einstein came back in nineteen
thirty with his

101
00:04:32,153 --> 00:04:34,953
cleverest challenge: a box full
of light,

102
00:04:35,393 --> 00:04:38,803
hanging from a spring scale,
with a clock inside that

103
00:04:38,813 --> 00:04:41,253
opens a shutter to let out a
single photon.

104
00:04:42,250 --> 00:04:44,610
By weighing the box before and
after, he argued,

105
00:04:44,790 --> 00:04:47,760
you could know both the exact
energy of the photon and the
exact

106
00:04:47,770 --> 00:04:48,730
time it left.

107
00:04:49,090 --> 00:04:51,730
That would break Heisenberg's
uncertainty principle.

108
00:04:52,583 --> 00:04:53,383
Bohr was shaken.

109
00:04:53,823 --> 00:04:56,403
A colleague said he looked like
a man in shock.

110
00:04:57,250 --> 00:04:59,900
But after a sleepless night, he
found the flaw,

111
00:04:59,910 --> 00:05:03,270
using Einstein's own theory of
general relativity.

112
00:05:03,990 --> 00:05:05,330
When the box moves in gravity,

113
00:05:06,250 --> 00:05:08,710
its clock runs at a slightly
different rate.

114
00:05:09,270 --> 00:05:10,810
The uncertainty was restored.

115
00:05:12,567 --> 00:05:14,087
Einstein changed tactics.

116
00:05:14,867 --> 00:05:18,177
In nineteen thirty five, with
Boris Podolsky and

117
00:05:18,187 --> 00:05:18,987
Nathan Rosen,

118
00:05:19,587 --> 00:05:23,057
he published a paper arguing
that quantum mechanics must be

119
00:05:23,067 --> 00:05:23,867
incomplete.

120
00:05:24,500 --> 00:05:27,680
The theory predicts that two
particles can become entangled.

121
00:05:28,400 --> 00:05:31,870
Separate them, even by vast
distances, and measuring

122
00:05:31,880 --> 00:05:35,370
one instantly affects what you
will find when you measure

123
00:05:35,380 --> 00:05:36,180
the other.

124
00:05:36,500 --> 00:05:38,440
Einstein thought that was
absurd.

125
00:05:39,040 --> 00:05:41,720
He later called it spooky action
at a distance.

126
00:05:42,420 --> 00:05:45,890
Surely, he said, the particles
must have carried hidden

127
00:05:45,900 --> 00:05:47,240
instructions all along.

128
00:05:48,083 --> 00:05:51,333
Bohr replied that it makes no
sense to talk about properties

129
00:05:51,343 --> 00:05:52,803
that haven't been measured.

130
00:05:53,303 --> 00:05:56,463
For the rest of their lives,
neither man convinced the other.

131
00:05:57,167 --> 00:06:00,277
Einstein spent his last years
searching for a deeper

132
00:06:00,287 --> 00:06:02,347
theory that would make chance
disappear.

133
00:06:03,067 --> 00:06:06,507
He died in nineteen fifty five,
without finding it.

134
00:06:07,167 --> 00:06:08,827
Yet they remained deep friends.

135
00:06:09,507 --> 00:06:12,377
And when Bohr died in nineteen
sixty two,

136
00:06:12,387 --> 00:06:15,697
the last drawing on his
blackboard was a sketch of

137
00:06:15,707 --> 00:06:17,087
Einstein's light box.

138
00:06:18,900 --> 00:06:22,190
During the Second World War,
Bohr, whose mother was

139
00:06:22,200 --> 00:06:23,000
Jewish,

140
00:06:23,080 --> 00:06:26,310
fled Nazi-occupied Denmark by
fishing boat to

141
00:06:26,320 --> 00:06:28,327
Sweden in nineteen forty three.

142
00:06:28,833 --> 00:06:32,243
He was then flown to Britain
lying in the empty bomb bay of

143
00:06:32,253 --> 00:06:33,853
a fast Mosquito aircraft.

144
00:06:34,433 --> 00:06:37,243
His oxygen mask didn't fit his
large head,

145
00:06:37,253 --> 00:06:38,813
and he passed out on the way.

146
00:06:39,500 --> 00:06:42,330
Earlier, when the Nazis invaded
Denmark in

147
00:06:42,340 --> 00:06:45,120
nineteen forty, a chemist at
Bohr's institute,

148
00:06:45,820 --> 00:06:49,190
George de Hevesy, dissolved two
colleagues' gold Nobel

149
00:06:49,200 --> 00:06:51,260
medals in acid to hide them.

150
00:06:51,700 --> 00:06:54,350
After the war, the gold was
recovered and the medals

151
00:06:54,360 --> 00:06:55,291
recast.

152
00:06:55,750 --> 00:06:58,960
Bohr went on to Los Alamos,
under the false name

153
00:06:58,970 --> 00:07:02,000
Nicholas Baker, as an adviser on
the atomic

154
00:07:02,010 --> 00:07:02,810
bomb.

155
00:07:03,090 --> 00:07:06,220
He urged Churchill and Roosevelt
to share atomic

156
00:07:06,230 --> 00:07:09,610
secrets with the world, to avoid
an arms race.

157
00:07:10,610 --> 00:07:11,630
Churchill wanted him watched.

158
00:07:13,483 --> 00:07:16,943
For decades, the Bohr-Einstein
debate seemed like philosophy.

159
00:07:17,703 --> 00:07:19,543
Nobody could see how to test it.

160
00:07:20,167 --> 00:07:23,257
Then, in nineteen sixty four,
the Northern Irish

161
00:07:23,267 --> 00:07:25,807
physicist John Bell found a way.

162
00:07:26,187 --> 00:07:29,277
He showed that hidden
instructions and quantum
mechanics

163
00:07:29,287 --> 00:07:31,387
predict measurably different
results.

164
00:07:32,333 --> 00:07:34,943
Bell worked at the particle
physics lab CERN,

165
00:07:34,953 --> 00:07:35,893
near Geneva.

166
00:07:36,633 --> 00:07:39,233
He did this work on the side,
almost as a hobby,

167
00:07:39,753 --> 00:07:42,333
and it became one of the most
important results in physics.

168
00:07:43,250 --> 00:07:46,210
Experiments by John Clauser in
the nineteen

169
00:07:46,220 --> 00:07:46,880
seventies,

170
00:07:46,890 --> 00:07:49,680
and Alain Aspect in the nineteen
eighties,

171
00:07:49,690 --> 00:07:53,020
tested Bell's idea with
entangled particles of

172
00:07:53,030 --> 00:07:53,830
light.

173
00:07:54,000 --> 00:07:57,000
Again and again, quantum
mechanics won.

174
00:07:57,440 --> 00:08:00,420
The universe really is as
strange as Bohr said.

175
00:08:01,083 --> 00:08:04,553
In twenty twenty two, Aspect,
Clauser and

176
00:08:04,563 --> 00:08:07,733
Anton Zeilinger shared the Nobel
Prize in Physics

177
00:08:07,743 --> 00:08:08,563
for this work.

178
00:08:09,250 --> 00:08:12,450
Today, entanglement is no longer
just an argument.

179
00:08:13,010 --> 00:08:16,400
It's the basis of quantum
computers and ultra-secure

180
00:08:16,410 --> 00:08:17,470
quantum communication.

181
00:08:18,583 --> 00:08:22,013
In twenty seventeen, a Chinese
satellite called

182
00:08:22,023 --> 00:08:25,293
Micius sent entangled particles
of light to two ground

183
00:08:25,303 --> 00:08:28,403
stations more than twelve
hundred kilometres apart.

184
00:08:29,023 --> 00:08:32,333
They stayed linked, just as
Bohr's quantum mechanics

185
00:08:32,343 --> 00:08:33,143
predicted.

186
00:08:33,667 --> 00:08:35,967
When Denmark gave Bohr its
highest honour,

187
00:08:36,507 --> 00:08:39,497
he designed his own coat of
arms, with the Chinese

188
00:08:39,507 --> 00:08:41,807
yin-yang symbol and a Latin
motto:

189
00:08:42,427 --> 00:08:43,647
opposites are complementary.

190
00:08:44,667 --> 00:08:47,645
Element number one hundred and
seven,

191
00:08:47,655 --> 00:08:48,197
bohrium,

192
00:08:48,207 --> 00:08:49,467
is named after him.

193
00:08:49,907 --> 00:08:53,397
And the institute he founded in
Copenhagen still carries his

194
00:08:53,407 --> 00:08:54,207
name.

195
00:08:55,400 --> 00:08:56,900
Einstein lost the argument.

196
00:08:57,420 --> 00:09:00,890
But by pushing so hard, he
forced quantum mechanics to

197
00:09:00,900 --> 00:09:03,920
explain itself, and revealed its
strangest feature.

198
00:09:04,833 --> 00:09:08,203
Sometimes, the greatest gift a
scientist can give

199
00:09:08,213 --> 00:09:09,493
is a really good objection.
